  "narrative_body": "Friedrich Merz hosted Volodymyr Zelenskyy in Berlin this week for Government Consultations — Germany's highest bilateral diplomatic format, reserved for its closest partners.\n\nThe format matters. It typically pulls ministers together across portfolios — foreign, defence, economy, finance — and produces multi-year cooperation frameworks. Activating it with Ukraine now is a real commitment, not a symbolic one: Germany is not just providing military assistance but locking the relationship into its most durable diplomatic structure. What was agreed remains unclear; specific commitments, declaration text, and any new aid packages cannot be confirmed from available sources this week.\n\nEven as it engaged Ukraine at the highest level, the government signalled its economic intentions abroad. Berlin announced a package combining short-term crisis relief with structural reform and, notably, published a simultaneous French-language version — suggesting the announcement was aimed at EU partners or Francophone audiences rather than the domestic press. The pattern fits announcements timed to EU Council cycles or coordinated with France, Belgium or Luxembourg. What the package contains — which sectors, how much money — cannot be determined from available sources.\n\nBoth moves come against a fraught domestic backdrop. From the prior week: the Alternative for Germany had taken its first-ever polling lead over the Christian Democratic Union and Christian Social Union across multiple institutes, recording 26–27% against 23–25%; the chancellor's approval had fallen to 20–27% — the lowest of any tracked politician; and the coalition was showing friction between Lars Klingbeil, the finance minister, and Katherina Reiche, the economics minister, over the shape of the economic response. The IMF had cut its 2026 growth forecast to 0.8%. None of those readings has been corroborated or contradicted this week — they stand as the baseline.\n\nOne thread that had been active went quiet. Planning for a potential German naval contribution to a Hormuz maritime coalition — which Boris Pistorius, the defence minister, had been advancing through partner and capability studies — produced nothing new this week. Whether that reflects a genuine pause or a gap in available sources cannot be determined. The prior assessment that operational planning is under way stands.",
